      "message": "HFP v1.9 AT+BRSF could be 32-bit integer, but the maximum number of bits that we can use is 11 bits, which is within int16. \n\nAnd we can see function `utl_str2int` can handle extra leading zeros. For example, in this code, it can output 4097 instead of -1, so I am wondering, how extra leading zero would cause this issue\n\n```\n#include \u003ciostream\u003e\n\n\nint16_t utl_str2int(const char* p_s) {\n  int32_t val \u003d 0;\n\n  for (; *p_s \u003d\u003d \u0027 \u0027 \u0026\u0026 *p_s !\u003d 0; p_s++)\n    ;\n\n  if (*p_s \u003d\u003d 0) return -1;\n\n  for (;;) {\n    if ((*p_s \u003c \u00270\u0027) || (*p_s \u003e \u00279\u0027)) return -1;\n\n    val +\u003d (int32_t)(*p_s++ - \u00270\u0027);\n\n    if (val \u003e 32767) return -1;\n\n    if (*p_s \u003d\u003d 0) {\n      return (int16_t)val;\n    } else {\n      val *\u003d 10;\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nint main() {\n    // Write C++ code here\n    std::string test\u003d\"000004097\"; // 2^12+1\n    const char* p_s \u003d test.c_str();\n    int result \u003d utl_str2int(p_s);\n    std::cout \u003c\u003c \"result: \"\u003c\u003cresult;\n\n    return 0;\n}\n\n```",
